# Production request routing — BEHIND an existing host nginx.
#
# TOPOLOGY (changed 2026-07-29). nginx on the host owns :80 and :443 and is the
# TLS terminator; this Caddy no longer does either:
#
#   browser --TLS--> host nginx :443 --plain HTTP--> caddy 127.0.0.1:8080
#                                                      |-- frontend:5173
#                                                      `-- backend:8000
#
# Caddy's job is now ONLY hostname routing (which of the two services) plus the
# legacy plain-HTTP redirects. It issues no certificates: every site block below
# is `http://`-scoped, which tells Caddy to serve plain HTTP and never attempt
# ACME. There is consequently no `email` / ACME_EMAIL setting any more — see
# docker-compose.prod.yml.
#
# The `caddy_data` volume is retained. It no longer holds Let's Encrypt certs,
# but Caddy still writes state there; removing it is a separate change.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FORWARDED HEADERS ARE LOAD-BEARING. Both lines in each block below fix a
# specific production failure, and both are consequences of nginx (not Caddy)
# now being the TLS terminator:
#
# 1. `header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https`
#    The nginx -> Caddy hop is plain HTTP, so Caddy would otherwise pass
#    X-Forwarded-Proto: http to the backend. uvicorn's ProxyHeadersMiddleware
#    rewrites scope["scheme"] from that header, and app/storage.py:media_url
#    builds EVERY private-asset URL from request.base_url (~16 call sites: brand
#    logo, hat-type angles, company graphics, blank-hat composites, session
#    view_images, admin thumbnails, quote components). Get this wrong and all of
#    them come back http://, the browser blocks them as mixed content on the
#    HTTPS page, and the studio renders with no imagery at all.
#
#    Setting it explicitly here (rather than relying on Caddy to relay nginx's
#    value) is deliberate: it is deterministic and does not depend on Caddy's
#    trusted_proxies defaults, which govern whether an inbound X-Forwarded-*
#    header from an untrusted peer is honoured or discarded.
#
# 2. `header_up X-Forwarded-For {http.request.header.X-Real-IP}`
#    {remote_host} — what this file used to send — is now the address of the
#    HOST NGINX, not the customer. Sending it would put every customer in one
#    rate-limit bucket (ProxyHeadersMiddleware recovers the client IP from
#    X-Forwarded-For, trusting the LEFTMOST entry), so everyone starts getting
#    429s under mild load.
#
#    X-Real-IP is used as the carrier — NOT X-Forwarded-For — precisely because
#    Caddy applies its own X-Forwarded-For handling (appending, and discarding
#    unsolicited inbound values from untrusted peers). X-Real-IP is outside that
#    machinery, so it arrives verbatim and the placeholder is reliable.
#
#    THE TRUST BOUNDARY IS NOW NGINX. This is only safe because the nginx config
#    sets `proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr` — $remote_addr is the real
#    peer address and REPLACES anything the client sent. If that line is ever
#    changed to relay a client-supplied value, an attacker can hand themselves a
#    fresh rate-limit bucket per request. See nginx/madhats.conf.example.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

http://madhats.getaiconsult.com.au {
	reverse_proxy frontend:5173 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {http.request.header.X-Real-IP}
	}
}

http://api.madhats.getaiconsult.com.au {
	reverse_proxy backend:8000 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {http.request.header.X-Real-IP}
	}
}

# --- Legacy plain-HTTP ports: redirect-only, temporary ------------------------
# Verification and quote links ALREADY DELIVERED to customers point at
# http://madhats.getaiconsult.com.au:8000/... with signed tokens. Dropping these
# ports would dead-end every one of them. {uri} preserves path AND query, so the
# tokens survive the redirect.
#
# These stay bound DIRECTLY to the host (docker-compose.prod.yml still publishes
# 8000 and 5173 publicly) rather than going through nginx: nginx owns 80/443
# only, and these two ports are unclaimed. They are address-only blocks, so
# Caddy serves them over plain HTTP and attempts no certificate.
#
# REMOVE once outstanding tokens have expired: the longest-lived is the quote
# token at QUOTE_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS = 2592000 (30 days).

:8000 {
	redir https://api.madhats.getaiconsult.com.au{uri} permanent
}

:5173 {
	redir https://madhats.getaiconsult.com.au{uri} permanent
}
